cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use this software or results, please cite the supplement and
  the upstream references below.
title: >-
  Does a Multi-Agent Group's Confidence Distribution Predict Task Difficulty?
  A Pre-registered, Provenance-Complete Test on Perturb-seq Response
  Prediction in Which Entropy Fails Its Criterion and the Confidence Drop
  Passes
# GENERATED from paper/paper.tex's abstract by scripts/paper/render_publish_description.py -- do not edit by hand.
abstract: >-
  A multi-agent group-generation system, as operated here, spends test-time
  compute uniformly across tasks. That a single agent's own vote distribution
  can gate its test-time compute is already reported by CATTS; we ask the
  multi-agent question: whether the distribution of stated confidences and
  critiques across a group of role-specialised agents predicts how hard a task
  is, which would let the group's compute be allocated adaptively. Taking as
  testbed a five-agent orchestrator for Perturb-seq response prediction
  inspired by CellForge and operated via MassGen, we define four trace metrics
  — Agent Confidence Entropy (ACE), Critique Severity Dispersion (CSD), a
  convergence signal (ΔC), and a Winner Flip Rate (WFR) — and their composite,
  the Task Difficulty Index (TDI). We pre-register five hypotheses with
  criteria: attainable error on Adamson 2016 (CRISPR interference) and Norman
  2019 (CRISPR activation), the Architect agent's backbone-choice entropy, the
  rank correlation of each TDI component with held-out error, and
  cross-dataset transfer of a calibrated TDI. The design is 41 held-out K562
  tasks (21 Adamson, 15 Norman singletons, 5 Norman doublets) drawn
  deterministically, a trainer sweep of 27 records per task and three
  fixed-length agentic lifecycle runs per task, all in one process. The agents
  are large language models (LLMs) from a pinned roster of Anthropic Claude
  models (Haiku 4.5 for the four proposer roles, Sonnet 5.5 for the Validator)
  whose served model is recorded and asserted per call; a run with any
  rule-based fallback step is invalid. Every run records its git revision,
  resolved configuration, library versions, dataset SHA-256 digests, task plan
  and label contract. We find that four of the five pre-registered hypotheses
  are supported and the cross-dataset transfer hypothesis is not; every
  hypothesis could be evaluated. The trainer sweep reaches the pre-registered
  error on both screens, and the Architect's choice of backbone varies above
  the pre-registered entropy floor. Within each screen, the drop in the
  agents' confidence across rounds, and the composite that inherits it, rank
  tasks by held-out error. From the second round on, however, every agent's
  prompt carries the previous round's held-out error, so that drop is stated
  after an error has been shown. In an exploratory analysis, the confidence
  stated before any feedback does not rank tasks by error (ρ = 0.003 on
  Adamson and 0.214 on Norman, both intervals including zero). The supported
  result is therefore that stated confidence falls further where a larger
  error was shown, not that the group's confidence anticipates difficulty. The
  composite fitted on Adamson did not meet the pre-registered transfer
  criterion on Norman (ρ = 0.286 against a criterion of > 0.4). The point
  estimates for confidence entropy, an evenness index over the agents, run
  against the pre-registered direction in both screens; this is exploratory,
  and no claim is made. The preflight probe and recommender are specified but
  not evaluated. The values reported in the earlier release of this work are
  superseded in full; the defects behind each are listed in the appendix.
